WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.011
<v Examiner>The three regions of the ear.

2
00:00:03.011 --> 00:00:04.226
<v Examiner>Four and a half marks.

3
00:00:04.676 --> 00:00:06.683
<v Examiner>What are the three regions of the ear?

4
00:00:06.683 --> 00:00:09.113
<v Examiner>Describe their structures and their functions.

5
00:00:09.563 --> 00:00:12.935
Four and a half marks means about nine half-mark facts.

6
00:00:12.935 --> 00:00:14.897
Three regions, three facts each.

7
00:00:15.347 --> 00:00:20.497
The hook: the outer ear collects, the middle ear amplifies, and the inner ear senses.

8
00:00:20.497 --> 00:00:25.949
And the inner ear does two jobs: the cochlea hears, and the vestibular apparatus balances.

9
00:00:26.399 --> 00:00:28.370
The outer ear, or external ear.

10
00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:33.964
The pinna, the external auditory canal, and the tympanic membrane, which is the eardrum.

11
00:00:33.964 --> 00:00:37.143
Glands in the canal make cerumen, which is earwax.

12
00:00:37.143 --> 00:00:41.211
Its function: it collects sound and directs it onto the eardrum,

13
00:00:41.211 --> 00:00:45.343
and the pinna also helps you locate where a sound is coming from.

14
00:00:45.793 --> 00:00:46.703
The middle ear.

15
00:00:46.703 --> 00:00:51.373
An air-filled cavity in the temporal bone, holding the ossicles: the malleus,

16
00:00:51.373 --> 00:00:52.889
the incus and the stapes.

17
00:00:52.889 --> 00:00:54.890
Its function: it amplifies sound,

18
00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:58.893
passing it from the large eardrum to the much smaller oval window.

19
00:00:58.893 --> 00:01:03.198
It also has the Eustachian tube, also called the pharyngotympanic tube,

20
00:01:03.198 --> 00:01:06.413
running to the nasopharynx, which equalises pressure.

21
00:01:06.413 --> 00:01:11.628
And two small muscles, the tensor tympani and the stapedius, which dampen loud sounds.

22
00:01:13.078 --> 00:01:13.960
The inner ear.

23
00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:17.927
The bony labyrinth in the temporal bone, filled with perilymph,

24
00:01:17.927 --> 00:01:21.831
and inside it the membranous labyrinth, filled with endolymph.

25
00:01:21.831 --> 00:01:22.901
It has two parts.

26
00:01:22.901 --> 00:01:28.380
The cochlea, for hearing: its organ of Corti turns sound vibration into nerve impulses.

27
00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:33.480
And the vestibular apparatus, which is the vestibule and the semicircular canals,

28
00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.243
for equilibrium, or balance.

29
00:01:36.693 --> 00:01:37.366
The traps.

30
00:01:37.366 --> 00:01:39.519
Only the inner ear does balance.

31
00:01:39.519 --> 00:01:42.613
The outer and middle ear are for hearing only.

32
00:01:42.613 --> 00:01:45.775
Second: the Eustachian tube equalises pressure.

33
00:01:45.775 --> 00:01:48.062
It's why your ears pop on a plane.

34
00:01:48.062 --> 00:01:49.609
It doesn't carry sound.

35
00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:53.511
Third: perilymph is in the bony labyrinth, on the outside.

36
00:01:53.511 --> 00:01:56.538
Endolymph is inside the membranous labyrinth.

37
00:01:56.538 --> 00:01:57.749
Endo means inside.

38
00:01:58.199 --> 00:01:58.746
<v Examiner>Once more.

39
00:01:58.746 --> 00:02:00.333
<v Examiner>The three regions of the ear.

40
00:02:02.783 --> 00:02:06.909
The outer ear: the pinna, the external auditory canal with its cerumen,

41
00:02:06.909 --> 00:02:08.420
and the tympanic membrane.

42
00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:11.617
It collects sound onto the eardrum and helps locate it.

43
00:02:11.617 --> 00:02:15.569
The middle ear: air-filled, in the temporal bone, with the ossicles,

44
00:02:15.569 --> 00:02:17.952
which amplify sound onto the oval window.

45
00:02:17.952 --> 00:02:22.660
The Eustachian tube equalises pressure, and two small muscles dampen loud sounds.

46
00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:27.775
The inner ear: a bony labyrinth of perilymph around a membranous labyrinth of endolymph.

47
00:02:27.775 --> 00:02:30.332
The cochlea, with its organ of Corti, hears.

48
00:02:30.332 --> 00:02:32.308
The vestibular apparatus balances.
